<h1>FTDI Suite</h1>
<p>The tutorial suite ran against the Maze application, a program, and the previous page moved to the network
	between machines. A test lab usually has hardware on the bench too: a board wired to the machine that talks to
	it, sometimes to two machines that have to act together. The <strong>FTDI GPIO</strong> example suite is a small suite of that kind. It drives the
	<strong>FT2232H Mini Module</strong>, an inexpensive USB board from FTDI, as a set of GPIO pins from the
	endpoints: one module on one machine blinks LEDs and reads its own pins back, and two modules on two machines,
	wired to each other, carry text messages across a handful of wires with a handshake.</p>
<p>The suite is a separate repository laid out exactly like the tutorial suite. Clone it next to the tutorial suite
	and add its entry point, <code>test_suite_ftdi.py</code>, to the controller the same way the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/setup/getting-started/">Getting started</a> guide adds the tutorial suite:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("git clone https://github.com/GMCoreAI/orcraft-test-suite-ftdi.git") }}
<p class="text-danger">This repository is currently private; the link above will be updated once it is public.</p>
<p>Beyond what the tutorial suite shows, this one demonstrates:</p>
<ul>
	<li><strong>Suite requirements</strong>: the Python packages the suite needs are installed on the endpoints
		by the controller, from a <code>requirements.txt</code> at the root of the repository.</li>
	<li><strong>One hardware handle for a whole test</strong>: the module is opened once in <code>_setup()</code>,
		kept open on the endpoint, handed to every step of every case, and closed in <code>_teardown()</code>.</li>
	<li><strong>Two endpoints coordinated at wire level</strong>: a receiver waits in the background on one
		machine while a sender drives the wires from the other, and the flow collects what arrived.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="hardware">Hardware</h2>
<p>The LEDs group needs one module on one endpoint; the GPIO Link group needs a second module on a second
	endpoint. The same wiring serves every test, so build it once:</p>
<table>
	<thead>
		<tr><th>Part</th><th>Quantity</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td>FT2232H Mini Module</td><td>1 or 2</td><td>One for the LEDs group, two for the GPIO Link group.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>USB mini-B cable</td><td>1 per module</td><td>The module is powered from USB.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>Breadboard</td><td>1 per module</td><td>Holds the LEDs and resistors.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>LED</td><td>6 per module</td><td>One on each of AD0 to AD5.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>Resistor, 330 to 470 ohm</td><td>6 per module, plus 6 for the link</td><td>One in series with each LED, and one in series with each wire between the modules.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>Jumper wires</td><td>a handful</td><td>Five short ones per module for its power jumpers, seven between the modules.</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="module-setup">Module setup</h2>
<p>The Mini Module has two 26-pin headers, <strong>CN2</strong> and <strong>CN3</strong>, and ships with nothing
	connected between its power rails. The chip enumerates on USB only once the USB 5 V (<strong>VBUS</strong>) is
	jumpered to the regulator input (<strong>VCC</strong>), and its pins only go high once the regulator's 3.3 V
	output (<strong>V3V3</strong>) is jumpered to every I/O bank supply input (<strong>VIO</strong>). Five short
	jumper wires do that:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/Diagrams/FTDI-Module-Jumpers.svg", "Schematic top view of the FT2232H Mini Module with headers CN2 and CN3 drawn pin by pin, the five power jumpers J1 to J5 drawn between VBUS and VCC and between the V3V3 and VIO pins, and the channel A pins AD0 to AD7 marked", "large") }}
<table>
	<thead>
		<tr><th>Jumper</th><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td>J1</td><td>CN3-1 (VBUS)</td><td>CN3-3 (VCC)</td><td>Powers the module from the USB port.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>J2</td><td>CN2-1 (V3V3)</td><td>CN2-11 (VIO)</td><td>Feeds an I/O bank from the 3.3 V regulator.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>J3</td><td>CN2-3 (V3V3)</td><td>CN2-21 (VIO)</td><td>Feeds an I/O bank from the 3.3 V regulator.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>J4</td><td>CN2-5 (V3V3)</td><td>CN3-12 (VIO)</td><td>Feeds an I/O bank from the 3.3 V regulator.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>J5</td><td>CN3-12 (VIO)</td><td>CN3-22 (VIO)</td><td>Chains the last I/O bank to the same supply.</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
<p>The suite tells the two mistakes apart. Without J1 the module never appears on the USB bus and the
	<strong>Verify Module</strong> step fails with <em>Module auto was not found on this endpoint</em>. Without
	J2 to J5 the module is found and opens, but every pin stays low whatever is written to it, so
	<strong>LED Blink</strong> fails with the pin not following the written level:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/examples/ftdi-suite/led-blink-unpowered-bank.png", "Log of an LED Blink run on a module without the V3V3 to VIO jumpers: the test result lists eight failed cases and the failures table shows every case of pin 0 to 3 with the description Pin did not follow the written level", "large") }}

<h3 id="channel-a-pins">Channel A pins</h3>
<p>The suite uses channel A of the chip only, the pins printed <strong>AD0</strong> to <strong>AD7</strong> on
	CN2; channel B stays free. The pin numbers zigzag because the odd pins sit in one row of the header and the
	even pins in the other:</p>
<table>
	<thead>
		<tr><th>Signal</th><th>CN2 pin</th><th>LEDs group</th><th>GPIO Link group</th></tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td><code>AD0</code></td><td>7</td><td>LED 1</td><td>DATA0</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>AD1</code></td><td>10</td><td>LED 2</td><td>DATA1</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>AD2</code></td><td>9</td><td>LED 3</td><td>DATA2</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>AD3</code></td><td>12</td><td>LED 4</td><td>DATA3</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>AD4</code></td><td>14</td><td>LED 5</td><td>REQ</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>AD5</code></td><td>13</td><td>LED 6</td><td>ACK</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>AD6</code>, <code>AD7</code></td><td>16, 15</td><td>unused</td><td>unused</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>GND</code></td><td>2, 4 or 6</td><td>LED return</td><td>common ground</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="wiring">Wiring</h2>
<p>Every test runs on one wiring. The LEDs group uses a module on its own; the GPIO Link group joins two such
	modules with straight wires, and the same LEDs then show the traffic. Build the LED side on each module
	first, then join the modules.</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/examples/ftdi-suite/bench-setup.jpg", "The bench setup: two FT2232H Mini Modules on their USB cables behind a breadboard that carries the six LEDs in a row with their series resistors, three of the LEDs lit in the middle of a transfer", "large") }}

<h3 id="leds-on-one-module">LEDs on one module</h3>
<p>From each of <code>AD0</code> to <code>AD5</code>, an LED with its own series resistor to <code>GND</code>: the
	LED's anode to the pin, its cathode through the resistor to ground. A freshly plugged module sits in its UART
	mode, in which <code>AD0</code>, <code>AD2</code> and <code>AD4</code> idle high, so LEDs 1, 3 and 5 are lit
	until the suite opens the module; that is expected. The suite closes the module with every pin as an input, so
	after a run all LEDs are dark.</p>

<h3 id="link-between-two-modules">Link between two modules</h3>
<p>The two modules are wired pin to pin, <code>AD0</code> to <code>AD0</code> and so on up to <code>AD5</code>,
	plus the two grounds joined. Which side drives which wire follows who is sending, so the same wires carry
	messages both ways:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/Diagrams/FTDI-GPIO-Link.svg", "Wiring diagram of the test module on the test endpoint and the aux module on the aux endpoint: AD0 to AD5 run straight across through a series resistor each, DATA0 to DATA3 and REQ driven by the sender, ACK driven by the receiver, an LED with its own resistor from every signal wire to ground, and a dashed common ground wire", "large") }}
<table>
	<thead>
		<tr><th>Test module</th><th>Aux module</th><th>Signal</th><th>Driven by</th></tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td><code>AD0</code> to <code>AD3</code></td><td><code>AD0</code> to <code>AD3</code></td><td>DATA, one nibble</td><td>the sending side</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>AD4</code></td><td><code>AD4</code></td><td>REQ, a nibble is ready</td><td>the sending side</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>AD5</code></td><td><code>AD5</code></td><td>ACK, nibble taken</td><td>the receiving side</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>GND</code></td><td><code>GND</code></td><td>common ground</td><td>always</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
<p>Put a 330 to 470 ohm resistor in series with each of the six signal wires. A freshly plugged module drives
	<code>AD0</code>, <code>AD2</code> and <code>AD4</code> high in its UART mode, and until the suite opens both
	modules two outputs can face each other on one wire; the resistor keeps that clash under 10 mA, which the pins
	tolerate, while an input still reads the level cleanly. The LEDs on the wires make a transfer visible: the four
	data LEDs show each nibble as it lands and the REQ and ACK LEDs show the handshake. At a slow pace the message
	can be read off the board.</p>

<h2 id="endpoint-preparation">Endpoint preparation</h2>
<p>An endpoint that runs this suite needs two things the tutorial suite never asked for: Python packages that talk
	to the module, and an operating system that lets those packages reach the USB device.</p>

<h3 id="suite-requirements">Suite requirements</h3>
<p>The suite drives the module through <code>pyftdi</code>, which speaks to the chip over libusb. Windows ships no
	libusb, so the suite also lists <code>libusb-package</code>, a wheel that carries the library. Both are named in
	the <code>requirements.txt</code> at the root of the repository:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('pyftdi
libusb-package', 'text') }}
<p>Nothing is installed by hand. After the suite package is uploaded to an endpoint, the controller has the
	endpoint application's Python install the file with pip, once per uploaded package, and reports the outcome in
	the log next to the upload lines. The mechanism is described in the framework's
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/#suite-requirements">Suite requirements</a> section. A test
	that runs on <code>localhost</code> uses the controller's own Python, where the packages must be installed by
	hand.</p>

<h3 id="winusb-driver-on-windows">WinUSB driver on Windows</h3>
<p>When a Mini Module is plugged into a Windows machine, Windows binds FTDI's own serial driver to it and offers
	two COM ports. That driver does not let libusb open the device, so the first run on such an endpoint stops at
	the <strong>Verify Module</strong> step:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/examples/ftdi-suite/verify-module-needs-winusb.png", "Verify Module step result in the log with status FAIL and the failure description The module cannot be opened through libusb, on Windows switch its interfaces to the WinUSB driver with Zadig, on Linux give the user access to the USB device", "large") }}
<p>The fix is a one-time driver switch with <a class="text-accent" href="https://zadig.akeo.ie/">Zadig</a>, a
	small tool that replaces the driver of one USB interface with WinUSB, the generic driver libusb works with. On
	the endpoint machine, with the module plugged in:</p>
<ol>
	<li>Download and start Zadig. It needs no installation.</li>
	<li>Open <strong>Options</strong> and tick <strong>List All Devices</strong>, so devices that already have a
		driver are shown.</li>
	<li>Pick <strong>Dual RS232-HS (Interface 0)</strong> in the device list; the USB ID reads
		<code>0403 6010</code>.</li>
	<li>Leave <strong>WinUSB</strong> as the target driver and click <strong>Replace Driver</strong>.</li>
	<li>Pick <strong>Dual RS232-HS (Interface 1)</strong> and click <strong>Replace Driver</strong> again.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Switch both interfaces, not only Interface 0.</strong> The suite uses channel A, but the two channels
	of one module are two interfaces of one USB device, and libusb opens the device as a whole. A module with one
	interface on WinUSB and the other still on the FTDI driver does not always enumerate the same way after a
	replug or a reboot, and then the open fails again with the same message. With both on WinUSB the module is
	stable.</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/examples/ftdi-suite/zadig-replace-driver.png", "Zadig with Dual RS232-HS (Interface 0) selected, the current driver FTDIBUS on the left, WinUSB as the target driver on the right, and the Replace Driver button", "large") }}
<p>Zadig takes a few seconds per interface. When an interface is selected again afterwards, both sides show WinUSB
	and the button reads <strong>Reinstall Driver</strong>, which is how a prepared machine looks for Interface 0
	and for Interface 1 alike:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/examples/ftdi-suite/zadig-winusb-installed.png", "Zadig with Dual RS232-HS (Interface 0) selected after the switch, WinUSB shown as both the current and the target driver, and the button reading Reinstall Driver", "large") }}
<p>Run the test again; it now opens the module. The switch holds across reboots and applies to that module on that
	machine, so a second module or a second machine needs its own pass. The module no longer appears as a COM port
	while it has the WinUSB driver; to give it back to FTDI's driver, uninstall the device in the Device Manager
	with its driver and plug it in again.</p>

<h3 id="usb-access-on-linux">USB access on Linux</h3>
<p>Linux needs no driver change, because <code>pyftdi</code> detaches the kernel's serial driver from the
	interface by itself. What it needs is permission: by default only root may open a USB device, and the endpoint
	application runs as an ordinary user. A udev rule opens the FTDI devices to every user:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0403", ATTRS{idProduct}=="6010", MODE="0666"', 'text') }}
<p>Save it as <code>/etc/udev/rules.d/99-ftdi.rules</code>, reload the rules and plug the module in again:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger") }}

