	test_group_link/
		test_wire_map/
		test_send_message/
		test_receive_message/
		test_exchange_messages/', 'text') }}

<h3 id="one-handle-for-the-whole-test">One handle for the whole test</h3>
<p>Source file:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-ftdi/common/module_test.py") }}
<p>Opening a USB device is slow and disturbs the pins, so a test does it once. The base class of the LEDs tests
	opens the module in <code>_setup()</code>, keeps the handle for every case, and closes it in
	<code>_teardown()</code>. No step opens or closes the module itself:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('class ModuleTest(OrcraftTest):
	direction = 0x00

	def __init__(self, config: ModuleTestConfig) -> None:
		super().__init__(config)
		self.gpio = None

	def _setup(self) -> None:
		flow = FlowOpenModule(
			FlowOpenModuleConfig(
				endpoint=self.config.test_endpoint,
				module_serial=self.config.module_serial,
				direction=self.direction,
			)
		)
		flow_result = flow.run()

		# Raising from _setup aborts the test: no cases run and _teardown is skipped.
		if self.handle_errors(flow_result, self.result):
			raise RuntimeError(self.result.error_description or "Setup could not open the module.")

		self.gpio = flow_result.gpio

	def _teardown(self) -> None:
		flow = FlowCloseModule(
			FlowCloseModuleConfig(
				endpoint=self.config.test_endpoint,
				gpio=self.gpio,
			)
		)
		flow.run()') }}
<p>The handle never leaves the endpoint. The <strong>Open Module</strong> step creates it there and puts it on its
	result, and from then on the test passes that object to every step that targets the same endpoint, the way it
	passes any other value. What travels to the controller is a reference to the object, valid for as long as the
	test holds the endpoint, so a later step receives the very same open handle and works with it directly:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_flow(self) -> None:
	step_1_result = self.run_step(
		StepBlinkLed,
		endpoint=self.config.endpoint,
		gpio=self.config.gpio,
		pin=self.config.pin,
		period_ms=self.config.period_ms,
		blinks=self.config.blinks,
	)

	self.result.blinks_done = step_1_result.blinks_done

	self.handle_errors(step_1_result, self.result)') }}
<p>A step that needs the pins the other way round, an input where the previous step drove an output, changes the
	direction on the open handle instead of reopening it. That is what lets the link tests turn a module from the
	receiving side into the sending side between two flows without touching the wiring.</p>

<h2 id="leds-group">LEDs group</h2>
<p>Both tests of the group take one endpoint and one module. Select the test, set <strong>Test Endpoint</strong> to
	the machine the module is plugged into and leave <strong>Module Serial</strong> at <code>auto</code>, which
	takes the first FT2232H found on that machine. With two modules on one machine, the log of the
	<strong>Verify Module</strong> step lists every module it found, by serial number or, for a module with a
	blank EEPROM, by its USB position such as <code>bus 1 address 2</code>; either name can be entered as the
	serial.</p>

<h3 id="led-blink">LED Blink</h3>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-ftdi/test_group_leds/test_led_blink/") }}
<p>The test opens the module with every pin as an output and blinks one LED per case, with a matrix of the pin and
	the period:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('blinks = OrcraftConfigField.Numeric(default=5, label="Blinks")

matrix = OrcraftConfigField.Matrix(
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("pin", (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5)),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("period_ms", (200, 500)),
)') }}
<p>Each case drives its pin high and low for the number of blinks and reads the pin back after every write. The
	LED on the bench blinks, and the case passes when the module reported the level it was told to set every
	time. That read-back is what turns a demonstration into a test: the failure at the top of this page, a module
	whose I/O banks had no power, was found by it.</p>

<h3 id="led-pattern">LED Pattern</h3>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-ftdi/test_group_leds/test_led_pattern/") }}
<p>The second test drives all six LEDs at once and walks patterns over them, one sequence per case at one of two
	periods: a <em>walking one</em> lights each LED in turn, a <em>walking zero</em> darkens each in turn, and a
	<em>count</em> shows the numbers 0 to 63 in binary. Every pattern is read back after it is written, exactly as
	in LED Blink.</p>

<h2 id="gpio-link-group">GPIO Link group</h2>
<p>The link group needs both modules. Every test takes the <strong>Test Endpoint</strong> and the
	<strong>Aux Endpoint</strong>, the two machines, a <strong>Module Serial</strong> for each, the
	<strong>Message</strong> to send and a <strong>Pace ms</strong> that holds each nibble on the wires a little
	longer so the LEDs stay readable; set it to 0 for the fastest transfer. Both modules are opened in
	<code>_setup()</code> with every pin as an input, so nothing drives the shared wires until a flow assigns the
	roles.</p>
<p>A message crosses the wires one nibble at a time, with a four-phase handshake:</p>
<ol>
	<li>The sender puts the nibble on DATA0 to DATA3 and raises REQ.</li>
	<li>The receiver reads the nibble and raises ACK.</li>
	<li>The sender lowers REQ.</li>
	<li>The receiver lowers ACK, and the sender may put the next nibble on the wires.</li>
</ol>
<p>Each machine polls its USB port at its own speed, and the handshake means neither side can outrun the other. A
	frame is one length byte followed by the UTF-8 bytes of the message, so the receiver knows when the message is
	complete.</p>

<h3 id="wire-map">Wire Map</h3>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-ftdi/test_group_link/test_wire_map/") }}
<p>Run this test first, before any message. One module raises each of <code>AD0</code> to <code>AD5</code> in
	turn while the other reads all its pins, then the roles swap. A correct link maps every pin to the same pin on
	the other side and nothing else, and the test's <strong>Wire Map</strong> result spells the mapping out, pin by
	pin, in both directions:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('Test to aux: AD0 -> AD0; AD1 -> AD1; AD2 -> AD2; AD3 -> AD3; AD4 -> AD4; AD5 -> AD5
Aux to test: AD0 -> AD0; AD1 -> AD1; AD2 -> AD2; AD3 -> AD3; AD4 -> AD4; AD5 -> AD5', 'text') }}
<p>A swapped, missing or shorted wire shows up by name: a missing wire maps to <em>nothing</em>, a swapped pair
	maps each pin to the other, and a short between two pins of the driving module is reported as that module
	raising its own second pin.</p>

<h3 id="send-receive-and-exchange">Send, Receive and Exchange</h3>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-ftdi/test_group_link/test_send_message/") }}
<p>The three message tests share one flow, <strong>Transfer Message</strong>, and differ only in who sends.
	<strong>Send Message</strong> sends from the test endpoint to the aux endpoint, <strong>Receive Message</strong>
	the other way round, and <strong>Exchange Messages</strong> runs the flow twice on the same two handles: the
	message one way, then a <strong>Reply</strong> back. The flow has three steps:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_flow(self) -> None:
	# Step 1: start listening on the receiver; the step returns at once
	step_1_result = self.run_step(
		StepStartReceiver,
		endpoint=self.config.receiver_endpoint,
		gpio=self.config.receiver_gpio,
		timeout_sec=RECEIVE_TIMEOUT_SEC,
	)

	if self.handle_errors(step_1_result, self.result):
		return

	# Step 2: send the message from the sender
	step_2_result = self.run_step(
		StepSendMessage,
		endpoint=self.config.sender_endpoint,
		gpio=self.config.sender_gpio,
		message=self.config.message,
		pace_ms=self.config.pace_ms,
	)

	# Step 3: collect what the receiver got, even when the sender reported trouble
	step_3_result = self.run_step(
		StepWaitReceiver,
		endpoint=self.config.receiver_endpoint,
		task=step_1_result.task,
		duration_sec=WAIT_AFTER_SEND_SEC,
	)') }}
<p>The receiver has to be listening before the first nibble arrives, so the flow starts it first.
	<strong>Start Receiver</strong> makes its handle the receiving side, ACK as the only output, starts a thread
	on the aux endpoint that waits for REQ, and returns at once with the running task on its result, the pattern
	from <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/asynchronous-steps/">Handling Asynchronous Steps</a>.
	<strong>Send Message</strong> then makes the other handle the sending side, DATA and REQ as outputs, and
	drives the message. <strong>Wait Receiver</strong> goes back to the aux endpoint with the task, waits for the
	thread to finish and reads the message it collected. Select <strong>Send Message</strong>, set the two
	endpoints, click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong>, <strong class="text-purple">Run</strong>,
	and follow the flow in the log:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/examples/ftdi-suite/transfer-message-log.png", "Log of the Transfer Message flow with the Sender Endpoint GMG-TEST-1 and the Receiver Endpoint GMG-TEST-2, the Start Receiver step on GMG-TEST-2 passing at once with the line Receiver listening on ftdi://ftdi:2232h/1 for up to 60 s, and the Send Message step on GMG-TEST-1 passing with the checkpoint Message sent: 19 bytes in 2.116 s", "large") }}
<p>The sender reports 19 bytes for an 18-character message, the length byte plus the text, at a pace of 50 ms
	per nibble. The flow ends with a checkpoint that compares the received text with the sent one, and the test's
	result carries the <strong>Received Message</strong> and the <strong>Bytes Per Sec</strong>. A transfer that
	stalls fails with what the receiver had collected by then, nibble by nibble, so a wiring fault points at the
	wire.</p>
<p>With both machines prepared and the wiring in place, the whole suite runs green. Each LEDs test names the one
	machine its module is on, each link test names both, and the run panel on the right shows the same
	endpoint and module fields the tests were configured with:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/examples/ftdi-suite/full-suite-run.png", "Test Explorer with the FTDI GPIO suite fully passed: 22 planned, done and passed cases, the LEDs group with LED Blink and LED Pattern on GMG-TEST-1, the GPIO Link group with Wire Map, Send Message, Receive Message and Exchange Messages on GMG-TEST-1 and GMG-TEST-2, and the run panel of LED Blink on the right with the Test Endpoint, Module Serial auto, Blinks 5 and the twelve pin and period cases", "large") }}

<p>This suite is the shape of a lab test: hardware on the bench, driven from the machines it is plugged into,
	with the framework moving the code, the requirements and the results. An
	<a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/">AI agent</a> can extend it the same way it extends any suite, from
	the files the controller exports.</p>
